rooms_desc ({"You are standing before the gate of Lord Uglymouth's castle.The high portal is open and on top of the gatehouse blows aguard your arrival on a horn. The mounted guard of honour ridesout and posts itself in two rows, between which you can enterthe castle proper. At the horizon you can still see a dark spot,that must be the tower of the village church.","This is a pleasant courtyard. A crossroads of gravel paths leads fromthe central well. In the corners of the yard small gardens have beenplanted. South from here lies the gatehouse and to the west leads adoor to the keep. From the east the smell of well roasted meat comesblowing in the soft breeze. North, a horse studies you amusedly.","You have arrived at the stables of the castle. This area has beendivided into ten boxes, that can each house a horse comfortably.Since the guard of honour rode out, eight boxes are empty. Fromthe nineth box, a big, white warhorse looks amusedly at you. Thelast box has been converted into some kind of saddle chambre.","The smell of the oxen roasting over the fire makes you wish it wasalready time for dinner. Several tables and cupboards line the wallsof this room, filled with little pots and boxes. The fireplace andthe oven next to it dominate the south wall. To the north you canenter the servants' quarters. West lies the courtyard.","This is the Knights' Hall of the castle. It is a big room with longtables in the centre. At the west end stands the high table, whereLord Uglymouth is seated himself, when he's around. Two big chandeliersgive a pleasant bright light to the hall. Through the east door youarrive at the courtyard. North and west lie Uglymouth's private rooms.","This room is mostly occupied by unmade beds and clothes lingering allover the place. As you look carefully you see a small boy sleeping inone of the smaller beds. A washing bowl stands in the corner, severalhalf-dried towels hanging next to it. Obviously the less importantinhabitants of this castle have their quarters here.","This is Uglymouth's private office. A large oaken desk is standingin the centre of the room, with a comfortable leather chair behindand two wooden chairs before it. The floor is covered with a thickpersian carpet. A single window lets the daylight come through.A shelf on east wall is filled with big leatherbound books.","From the enormous bed you conclude that this is the Lord's bedroom.The bed is big and soft and carved with many details of heroism.An marble washing basin is build in the south wall, and a heavywardrobe stands against the west wall. You sink up to your anklesin a thick, warm carpet. A small door leads to a room north.","These are Lord Uglymouth's private quarters. He comes here when he wants tosit and think in quiet, apart from the busy life in the rest of the world.This room is very sparsely decorated. I simple field bed stands in onecorner, and a small wooden table against the wall. A big candle stands inthe centre of the room, providing a little light. For the rest it's empty.",0,})
